<h3>Thematic Concentration</h3>
<p>The exam content clusters into two dominant domains:</p>

<div class="tag-row">
  <span class="tag-item">C Programming Language</span>
  <span class="tag-item">Database Management Systems</span>
  <span class="tag-item">MS Access</span>
  <span class="tag-item">Control Structures</span>
  <span class="tag-item">Operators</span>
  <span class="tag-item">Functions</span>
  <span class="tag-item">Data Types</span>
  <span class="tag-item">Database Models</span>
  <span class="tag-item">Keys &amp; Relationships</span>
  <span class="tag-item">SQL/DML/DDL</span>
</div>

<p><strong>C Language Programming</strong> and <strong>Database Concepts</strong> together account for the overwhelming majority of exam content. Within C Programming, operators, expressions, format specifiers, control structures, and functions are the most tested concepts. Within Database, MS Access objects, database models, keys, and SQL language classifications (DDL, DML, DCL, TCL) dominate.</p>

<h3>Concept Evolution &mdash; When Did New Topics Appear?</h3>

<div class="chart-box">
  <div class="chart-title">Introduction of New Concepts Over Time (First Year Detected)</div>
  <canvas id="chartConceptEvolution"></canvas>
</div>

<p>The dataset reveals a clear pattern of <strong>curriculum modernization in recent years</strong>. Earlier papers (2015&ndash;2019) focused heavily on core C syntax, basic operators, and foundational database concepts. Starting in 2022, the exam began incorporating more advanced topics:</p>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Year Introduced</th><th>New Concepts Detected</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>2015&ndash;2016</td><td>Core C syntax, basic operators, database fundamentals, MS Access, strings</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2017&ndash;2018</td><td>Data dictionary, arrays, composite keys</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2019</td><td>Candidate keys, alternate keys</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2022</td><td><strong>Recursion, normalization</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>2024</td><td><strong>Pointers, macros, goto, data redundancy, indexes, referential integrity, Fibonacci series</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>2025</td><td><strong>Bitwise operators, super keys, object-oriented model, ternary operator, views, unions, entity integrity, type casting, binary search, storage classes, file handling</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>This late-period enrichment is significant: <strong>2024 and 2025 alone introduced more new advanced concepts than the entire 2015&ndash;2019 period combined.</strong> The exam in 2025 is demonstrably broader in conceptual scope than the 2015 exam, even though the question count is only slightly higher (33 vs. 27).</p>

<h3>Topic Distribution by Question Type</h3>

<div class="chart-box">
  <div class="chart-title">Topic Emphasis by Question Type (Percentage of Questions Within Each Type)</div>
